virglrenderer: only GL buffers, inside their size, at buffer binding points

What: a resource the guest binds as a vertex, index, uniform, stream output,
indirect, query, storage or atomic counter buffer must be a GL buffer object
(target PIPE_BUFFER with GL buffer storage). vrend checked only for a non-zero GL
name, so a texture's name was used as a buffer name. Ranges are checked in 64 bit
against the buffer: uniform ranges are clamped to the buffer's end; stream output
ranges start 4-byte aligned inside the buffer and are clamped; atomic counter
ranges and query results must fit; an indirect dispatch command must fit. The
emulated query-buffer path wrote into the GL buffer named like the query object
instead of the guest's buffer; it uses the buffer now.
Why: a name the GL does not know as a buffer leaves the previous binding in place
or makes the attribute a client array (address 0 + offset): the GPU then reads
memory no buffer owns. On 2026-10-04 a fuzzed command stream made the MacBook's
GPU fault on address 0 (BIF0 page fault); the GPU reset hung WindowServer and
macOS panicked. A guest must never reach that (whole-Mac denial of service).
Upstream: upstreamable hardening; not sent.
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-gpu-ranges.c (each refused binding, on Apple's software
renderer); Tests/virgl/fuzz-cmd-stream.sh with gl-oracle.c (aborts on any draw
whose buffers are unbound or too small).
